  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAnother period of tranquil and dry weather is expected this week as \nupper level ridging and a 1035mb surface high over the Ohio River \nValley region continues to build into the region with the core of \nthe high slowly sliding eastward over the Mid-Atlantic region before \nsitting and stalling there through Friday. After near seasonable \ntemperatures in the upper 40s and 50s today temperatures begin to \ngradually trend above normal through the weekend. The \ncombination of strong upper level height rises, unusually warm \n850mb temperatures in the 6 to 10 degrees C range, and \nwesterly/northwesterly flow advecting a warm and dry air mass \ninto the region will result in highs +5 to +10 degrees above \nnormal across the region this week. For perspective, the daily \nnormal high this time of year for the Albany area is 48 F. With \nthe peak of the ridge axis overhead near the end of the week, \nhighs Wednesday through Friday are expected to be in the upper \n40s reaching into the mid 50s for terrain areas, and in the \nupper 50s into the low 60s for the valley areas. Guidance shows \nFriday to be the warmest day with flow shifting out of the \nsouthwest. And while a weak upper level shortwave and low \npressure system passing to our north tracks through tomorrow \nafternoon, moisture remains too limited to overcome this strong \nsubsidence, but will result in increased cloud cover for areas \nnorth and west of Albany, especially in the higher terrain areas\nduring the first half of the day tomorrow. There will still be \nplenty of sunshine.\n\nLong range models show another subtle upper level shortwave \nassociated with an additional wave of weak low pressure to north \ncresting towards the trailing side of the high pressure ridge over \nour region on Friday. This along with a backdoor cold front \nbeginning to sink through into Saturday will again bring very little \nmoisture, likely just enough to increase cloud cover across areas \nnorth and west of I-90 on Friday and into the weekend. Given very \nweak cold air advection resulting from this front and the immediate \nrebuild of the antecedent ridge and surface high, temperatures \nSaturday only look to drop by a few degrees and will still remain \nabove normal in the 50s.",
